CAT = "iepsaRole"; SEG = "ROL"
# QQI 4N20604 Security Guarding Skills, learning outcomes 1.4, 1.5, 1.12, 1.16, 1.17; Equal Status
# Act 2000 s. 3; Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005 s. 13; Private Security Services Act
# 2004 s. 2 (definition of security guard).
Q = [
("A guard stands visibly at a shopping centre entrance in full uniform. Which security principle is this mainly?",
 "Prevention", ["Detection", "Investigation", "Prosecution"],
 "A visible, uniformed guard deters people from trying to commit a crime in the first place, which is prevention. Detection is spotting an incident that is happening or has happened."),
("Fitting a steel shutter and a stronger lock to a storeroom door is an example of:",
 "Protection", ["Detection", "Deterrence by patrol", "Incident reporting"],
 "Physical barriers such as locks, shutters and fences protect an asset by making it harder to reach. They do not tell anyone that an attack is happening."),
("A motion sensor triggers an alarm at the control room when someone enters a yard at night. This is:",
 "Detection", ["Prevention", "Protection", "Reception"],
 "Alarms, sensors and CCTV monitoring detect an intrusion so that someone can respond. A fence or lock that keeps the person out would be protection."),
("Which of these best describes the main purpose of a security guard?",
 "Protect people and property", ["Enforce criminal law like a Garda", "Collect debts for the client", "Punish anyone who breaks rules"],
 "The Act defines a security guard as a person who for remuneration guards, patrols or provides other protective services for persons or property. A guard is not a police officer and has no power to punish."),
("A client asks a guard to do something the guard believes is unlawful. What should he do?",
 "Refuse and tell his supervisor", ["Do it, the client pays", "Do it, then report it", "Walk off the site at once"],
 "A guard has no more legal powers than anyone else, and a client's instruction is no defence to an unlawful act. Refuse politely, stay on post and report it to the supervisor."),
("A visitor shouts at you about a parking rule you did not make. What is the best first step?",
 "Stay calm and listen", ["Raise your voice to match", "Walk off without a word", "Call the Gardaí straight away"],
 "Letting an angry person speak while you stay calm and listen lowers the temperature. Matching their volume escalates the conflict, and a parking complaint is not a police matter."),
("While calming an angry person, which body language helps most?",
 "Open palms, relaxed stance", ["Arms folded across the chest", "Pointing a finger at them", "Standing nose to nose"],
 "Open palms, a side-on relaxed stance and a safe distance signal that you are not a threat. Folded arms, pointing and closing the gap all read as hostile."),
("Why should a guard keep about an arm's length or more from an agitated person?",
 "Space and your own safety", ["To avoid hearing insults", "So the CCTV can film better", "Because touching is illegal"],
 "Keeping a safe distance respects personal space, which lowers tension, and gives you time to react if the person lashes out."),
("What is reflective listening when dealing with a complaint?",
 "Repeating back their point", ["Writing the complaint later", "Agreeing with every demand", "Looking away while they talk"],
 "Reflective listening means summarising the person's point in your own words so they know they have been heard. It calms people and checks you have understood."),
("A customer cannot speak English well and is lost. What should the guard do?",
 "Speak slowly, use gestures", ["Speak louder and faster", "Refuse to help them at all", "Send them to the Gardaí"],
 "Short sentences, a slower pace, pointing and maps help someone with little English. Raising your voice does not help understanding."),
("Under the Equal Status Act 2000, which of these is a discriminatory ground?",
 "Traveller community", ["Supporting a football club", "Wearing a particular brand", "Living in a certain county"],
 "Section 3(2) of the Equal Status Act lists the grounds, including gender, marital status, family status, sexual orientation, religion, age, disability, race and membership of the Traveller community."),
("A guard refuses a man entry to a shopping centre only because he is a Traveller. What is this?",
 "Unlawful discrimination", ["A normal security decision", "A matter for site rules only", "Lawful if the manager agrees"],
 "Treating someone less favourably because of membership of the Traveller community is discrimination under the Equal Status Act. A refusal must be based on behaviour, not on a protected ground."),
("Under section 13 of the Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005, a guard must not come to work:",
 "Under the influence", ["Without a packed lunch", "Without his own radio", "Earlier than his start time"],
 "Section 13(1)(b) says an employee must not be under the influence of an intoxicant to the extent that he or she endangers his or her own safety or that of others."),
("A guard notices a broken handrail on the stairs during a patrol. What does the 2005 Act expect of him?",
 "Report it promptly", ["Fix it himself with tape", "Ignore it, it is not security", "Mention it next week"],
 "Section 13(1)(h) requires an employee to report any defect in the place of work that might endanger anyone to the employer or another appropriate person as soon as practicable."),
("Your employer supplies a high-visibility jacket for car park duty. Under the 2005 Act you must:",
 "Use it correctly", ["Wear it only if it is dark", "Choose your own instead", "Keep it in the car"],
 "Section 13(1)(g) requires an employee to make correct use of protective clothing or equipment provided, in line with training and instructions."),
("Which appearance standard fits a professional guard reporting for duty?",
 "Clean, full uniform", ["Uniform jacket over jeans", "Any dark clothes", "Uniform with no badge"],
 "A clean, complete uniform tells the public who you are and that you can be trusted. A licence holder in uniform also wears the PSA identity badge where required."),
("A colleague on the same shift is struggling with a queue of visitors. What should you do?",
 "Help if your post allows", ["Leave your post at once", "Report him for being slow", "Ignore it, it is his job"],
 "Good teamwork means supporting colleagues without abandoning your own post. If your post must stay covered, call the supervisor."),
("Which of these is part of good customer care for a guard on reception?",
 "Greet visitors politely", ["Keep eyes on your phone", "Answer only staff", "Sit with your back to the door"],
 "A guard on reception is often the first person a visitor meets. A polite greeting, eye contact and help show professionalism and also let you observe everyone who arrives."),
("A guard posts photos of a client's alarm panel on social media. What has he mainly done wrong?",
 "Breached confidentiality", ["Breached copyright", "Nothing, if he is off duty", "Broken a traffic law"],
 "Details of a client's security systems are confidential. Sharing them helps criminals and breaches the guard's duty of confidentiality, on or off duty."),
("The site manager tells you to stop a woman leaving until she empties her bag, though nobody saw her take anything. What do you do?",
 "Explain you have no power", ["Hold her until she agrees", "Search the bag yourself", "Lock the exit doors on her"],
 "A guard has only the powers of any person. Holding someone with no arrestable offence committed is unlawful detention, whatever the client wants."),
("Which statement about a security guard's powers in Ireland is correct?",
 "Same arrest powers as anyone", ["Same powers as a Garda", "Extra powers from the PSA", "Power to issue on-the-spot fines"],
 "A PSA licence lets a person work in security; it does not add powers. A guard arrests under section 4 of the Criminal Law Act 1997, the power any person has."),
('A visitor in a wheelchair cannot use the main steps. What should the guard do?',
 'Show the accessible entrance', ['Ask them to come back later', 'Lift the chair up the steps', 'Tell them to phone ahead'],
 'Helping someone to the accessible entrance treats them with respect and meets the duty in section 4(1) of the Equal Status Act 2000 to do all that is reasonable to accommodate a person with a disability. Lifting a wheelchair risks injury to both of you.'),
("What is the best way to deal with a rude caller on the site phone?",
 "Stay polite, take details", ["Hang up immediately", "Be rude back to them", "Put them on hold for good"],
 "Staying polite, taking the caller's name, number and message and passing it on keeps you professional. Note any threat in your report."),
("A drunk man at a bus station is falling over but not causing harm. What is the guard's first concern?",
 "His welfare and safety", ["Getting him arrested", "Taking his photo", "Moving him off the property"],
 "A guard's role includes protecting people. A person too drunk to stand may be at risk of injury, so check on them and call for help if needed."),
]
